# What Wyrd solves

ML teams accumulate a coordination problem: the model in production is not the
model in the experiment log, the prompt that shipped is not the one in version
control, and the data the model trained on is a table name in someone's head.
Cards make these contracts explicit and durable.

## The coordination problem

Without a registry, every reference to a model, prompt, or dataset is informal —
a path, a name, a comment. Informal references do not version, do not validate,
and do not compose. When a policy or audit asks "what ran?", the answer is
reconstruction from logs.

With cards, every component has a typed identity. CardRefs are structural: the
server validates them and derives the lineage graph. "What ran?" becomes a query.

## What Wyrd governs

| Kind | What it captures |
| --- | --- |
| Data | Dataset identity, schema, interface, and stats |
| Model | Model artifact, provider contract, input/output shape |
| Prompt | Versioned prompt content and I/O contract |
| Agent | Declarative agent spec (runtime stays in Skald) |
| Workflow | Coordinated task graph over agents and operators |
| Eval | Scoring rule or acceptance check |
| … and 10 more | See [Cards overview](/cards/) for the full catalog |

The three shipped kinds (Data, Model, Prompt) are fully runnable today. The
remaining thirteen are reserved IA structure with authored contracts and JSON
Schemas but no runtime implementation yet.

